---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const squareCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'd1', label: 'distance 1', min: 50, max: 200, step: 5, value: 100, unit: 'cm' },
    { name: 'd2', label: 'distance 2', min: 50, max: 200, step: 5, value: 180, unit: 'cm' },
    { name: 'i1', label: 'intensity at distance 1', min: 1, max: 20, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'mGy' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'intensity at distance 2', expr: 'i1*d1*d1/(d2*d2)', unit: 'mGy' },
    { label: 'factor change', expr: 'd2*d2/(d1*d1)', unit: '×' },
  ],
};

const safetyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · X-rays are made by fast electrons', note: 'Boil electrons off a filament, slam them into a tungsten target at high voltage: the sudden deceleration emits X-rays (bremsstrahlung plus characteristic lines).' },
    { math: '2 · Tissue absorbs by density', note: 'Bone (calcium) absorbs far more than soft tissue, soft tissue more than air. That differential absorption is the entire image.' },
    { math: '3 · Dose is cumulative', note: 'Ionising damage adds up over a lifetime. Every exposure must be justified — benefit must outweigh the risk it adds.' },
    { math: '4 · Time, distance, shielding', note: 'Dose falls with the inverse square of distance and drops to near zero behind lead. The three cheapest protective devices in radiology.' },
    { math: '5 · ALARA', note: 'As Low As Reasonably Achievable — the operating principle of the whole profession: same diagnostic answer, minimum dose.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/radiography/foundational/x-ray-physics-and-safety">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Radiography is the discipline of making pictures with ionising radiation — safely. Its
    foundation is the physics of X-ray production and absorption, and its ethics is the ALARA
    principle: every exposure must earn its place by changing the diagnosis.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how X-rays are made, absorbed and justified" widget="equationStep" config={safetyCfg} />

  <h2>The inverse square law is your colleague</h2>
  <p>
    Radiation intensity falls with the square of distance: doubling your distance from the source
    cuts your dose to a quarter; scattering it further cuts it again. Students' hands learn this
    law before their notes do — distance is the cheapest shielding in the department. Try the
    calculator: moving a detector from 100 cm to 180 cm drops the needed beam intensity nearly
    four-fold for the same image.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="inverse square: intensity vs distance" widget="sliderLab" config={squareCfg} />

  <h2>The image is differential absorption</h2>
  <p>
    An X-ray image is a shadow map: tissues absorb the beam in proportion to their density and
    atomic number. Bone leaves a white shadow, air a black one, and the soft-tissue greys between
    them carry most of the diagnostic work. Modern digital detectors widened the exposure latitude
    enormously — but positioning skill still decides whether the anatomy you need is even in the beam.
  </p>

  <h2>Why radiographers, not just machines</h2>
  <p>
    The profession exists because physics meets people: anxious patients who cannot hold still,
    injuries that need special projections, children, and the constant judgement of exposure
    factors against body habitus. Radiation safety, patient care and image quality are one job —
    which is why the registry certifies a person, not a machine operator.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ARRT Radiography Content Specifications; Bushong, <em>Radiologic Science for Technologists</em>; ICRP dose recommendations.</p>
</TopicPage>
